# Citation metadata. GitHub reads this file and adds a "Cite this repository"
# button to the sidebar, which is how a dataset gets cited rather than
# screenshotted.
#
# TWO FIELDS NEED YOUR NAME BEFORE THIS IS USEFUL -- see the <<< markers.
# Everything else is filled in and correct.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or the facility dataset, please cite it."
type: software

title: "Siting Atlas: an open, reproducible model of where private infrastructure gets built"

abstract: >-
  An attempt to predict the siting of Amazon US last-mile delivery stations
  from free public data, and a pre-registered report that it does not work at
  either of the two geographic grains tested. Ships a parcel-level cost model
  that does work, a dataset of 1,904 facilities recovered by OCR from an
  image-only industry document, and a measured floor on the public-visibility
  gap: federal enforcement records hold an inspection in 138 of the 488 US
  cities where an independent census lists a delivery station.

authors:
  - family-names: "Dogiparthi"
    given-names: "Sharada"
    affiliation: "California State University, East Bay"

license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Dogiparthi-Sharada/siting-atlas"

keywords:
  - facility location
  - last-mile logistics
  - discrete choice
  - pre-registration
  - negative results
  - open data
  - reproducibility
  - urban analytics
  - data provenance

# Volume I. Bump this on each release and let Zenodo mint a DOI per version.
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-09-16"

# Once Zenodo has minted one, paste it here and it becomes the canonical
# citation. Zenodo links to GitHub in two clicks: zenodo.org -> GitHub -> flip
